//@version=6
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//   A T T Y   S T R A T E G Y   S E E K E R
//   © 2026 Atty Labs.
//
//   Searches a few hundred to a few thousand simple strategies, ranks them, and
//   then spends most of its panel explaining why the winner probably means
//   nothing. That second half is the product.
//
//   Every strategy search has the same three problems. This one answers all three
//   on screen instead of hiding them.
//
//   1. FILLS. A backtest that fills a stop AT the stop deletes its own left tail.
//      Measured 2026-09-12: a signal printing +$60/trade on NQ at t = +5.24 —
//      symmetric, stable across halves, consistent year by year, n = 1,566 — was
//      entirely this artefact. 175 of 876 entries opened already through the
//      level. Here a stop pays the WORSE of the level and the bar open, stop
//      exits pay extra slippage, and the gap-through rate is printed as a
//      first-class number rather than left invisible.
//
//   2. TIMING. A signal computed from a bar's close is not knowable until that
//      close prints, so the fill is the NEXT bar's open by default. Trailing
//      stops test the level carried in from the previous bar BEFORE ratcheting
//      on this bar's close — the reverse order is the most common intrabar
//      look-ahead in published strategy scripts and it always pays.
//
//   3. SELECTION. The best of N strategies is the maximum of N noisy draws, not
//      an estimate of anything. The panel prints what the best of N scores on
//      pure luck (Gumbel expected maximum and its 95% ceiling), what ALL N
//      strategies did, and how the top K held up on a frozen holdout that was
//      never used to rank them.
//
//   Nothing here is a performance claim. The leaderboard is labelled FITTED. The
//   only forward-looking numbers on the panel are the walk-forward basket and
//   the frozen holdout, and both are allowed to be ugly.
// ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
indicator("Atty Strategy Seeker", "ATTY SEEKER", overlay = false, max_bars_back = 1000, max_lines_count = 500, max_labels_count = 500, max_polylines_count = 8)

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 1 · SEARCH
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string gS = "1 · Search"
string sizeStr = input.string("Standard (768)", "Search size", options = ["Quick (288)", "Standard (768)", "Deep (1536)"], group = gS, tooltip = "Every strategy is entry signal x direction x exit rule x market filter. 24 entries, 8 exits, 6 filters. Bigger is not better: the larger the search, the higher the bar a winner must clear to mean anything, and the panel raises that bar for you.")
int lastN = input.int(2500, "Test the last N bars", minval = 400, maxval = 20000, group = gS, tooltip = "Older bars are ignored. Fewer bars load faster and prove less.")
int minTrades = input.int(30, "Minimum trades to qualify", minval = 10, maxval = 500, group = gS)
bool profOnly = input.bool(false, "Only profitable strategies may rank", group = gS, tooltip = "OFF by design. Turning it on hides every loser, and the share of the search that lost money is the most informative number on this panel. If half of a random search is profitable, a profitable champion tells you nothing.")
string dirMode = input.string("Long and short", "Directions", options = ["Long and short", "Long only", "Short only"], group = gS)
string rankBy = input.string("t-stat of mean return", "Rank strategies by", options = ["t-stat of mean return", "Profit factor", "Net profit", "Expectancy", "Return / drawdown", "Win % (weak)"], group = gS, tooltip = "t-stat = sqrt(trades) x mean(R) / stdev(R). It is the only entry on this list the luck bar can be computed against, which is why it is the default. Win % is offered for completeness and is the easiest metric here to top by chance.")
int followK = input.int(5, "Basket size K", minval = 1, maxval = 10, group = gS, tooltip = "The walk-forward basket equal-weights the K strategies that were top-ranked AT THE MOMENT each trade was entered. Following a single champion whipsaws.")
string basketMode = input.string("Top K by score", "Basket composition", options = ["Top K by score", "Best of each concept family"], group = gS, tooltip = "Top K by score takes the K best whatever they are, which frequently means five dressings of the same idea — two RSI(2) variants and three moving-average crosses diversify nothing. Best of each concept family instead takes the single best RANGE, BREAKOUT, TREND, MOMENTUM and SHOCK strategy, so the legs are picked to be structurally different rather than individually best. Selection is still walk-forward: each family champion is re-chosen bar by bar and a trade only counts for the basket if its strategy was already the family champion before the trade existed.")
float margin = input.float(25, "Champion hysteresis (%)", minval = 0, maxval = 200, step = 5, group = gS, tooltip = "A challenger takes the title only by beating the champion by this margin, so the crown does not flip on noise.")

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 2 · EXECUTION REALISM — every default here makes the result worse and truer
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string gX = "2 · Execution realism"
string execMode = input.string("Next bar open", "Fill entries at", options = ["Next bar open", "Signal bar close (optimistic)"], group = gX, tooltip = "A signal computed from a bar's close is not knowable until that close has printed. Filling AT it assumes a market-on-close order fired at the instant of the print. Next bar open is what you could actually have done. The optimistic setting exists so you can measure how much of an edge is this assumption alone.")
float slipTicks = input.float(1.0, "Slippage per side (ticks)", minval = 0, maxval = 100, step = 0.25, group = gX)
float stopSlip = input.float(2.0, "EXTRA slippage on stop exits (ticks)", minval = 0, maxval = 100, step = 0.25, group = gX, tooltip = "A stop is a marketable order fired into the move that triggered it. It slips more than anything else you do, and almost no backtest charges it for that.")
float commPct = input.float(0.01, "Commission per side (%)", minval = 0, maxval = 1, step = 0.005, group = gX)
bool gapCredit = input.bool(false, "Credit gap-through on targets", group = gX, tooltip = "OFF: a bar that gaps past a target fills AT the target, never at the better open. Stops always pay the worse of the level and the open whatever this is set to. The asymmetry is deliberate.")
string bothHit = input.string("Stop first (conservative)", "When one bar hits both stop and target", options = ["Stop first (conservative)", "Target first (optimistic)"], group = gX, tooltip = "A 5-minute or daily bar does not record which came first. Stop first is the honest default. Switch it to see how much of the result rests on an assumption the data cannot settle — the panel reports how many exits were ambiguous.")
int atrLen = input.int(14, "ATR length for stops and targets", minval = 2, maxval = 100, group = gX)
float atrScale = input.float(1.0, "Scale all stop and target distances", minval = 0.25, maxval = 4.0, step = 0.25, group = gX)
float trailMult = input.float(3.0, "Trailing stop distance (ATR)", minval = 0.5, maxval = 10.0, step = 0.5, group = gX)
bool flatEOD = input.bool(false, "Force flat at a fixed time each day (intraday only)", group = gX, tooltip = "Closes any open position at the first bar at or after the time below, and blocks new entries from that time on, so nothing carries overnight. This is what separates a genuinely intraday result from one that merely holds few bars: a 20-bar hold on a 5-minute chart is 100 minutes, but without this it can still straddle the bell. Deliberately a CLOCK and not Pine session built-ins — ES trades a near-24-hour symbol session, so those fire once a day at 17:00 even on a chart displaying regular hours only, and a force-flat built on them silently never triggers.")
string flatTz = input.string("America/New_York", "Exchange timezone", group = gX)
int flatHour = input.int(15, "Flat at (hour)", minval = 0, maxval = 23, group = gX, inline = "flat")
int flatMinute = input.int(45, "minute", minval = 0, maxval = 59, group = gX, inline = "flat")

// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// 3 · EVIDENCE
// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
string gE = "3 · Evidence"
int isPct = input.int(70, "In-sample share of the window (%)", minval = 30, maxval = 90, group = gE, tooltip = "The top K by in-sample score are FROZEN at this point and then tracked, with no re-ranking, across the remaining bars. That holdout figure is the closest thing on this panel to an out-of-sample result.")
bool showLuck = input.bool(true, "Luck bar — what the best of N scores on noise", group = gE)
bool showNull = input.bool(true, "The null — what all N strategies did", group = gE)
bool showFill = input.bool(true, "Fill-realism diagnostics", group = gE)
bool showVerdict = input.bool(true, "Verdict banner", group = gE)
bool showFam = input.bool(true, "Concept-family basket breakdown", group = gE)
int corrLen = input.int(1000, "Bars used for the leg-correlation estimate", minval = 100, maxval = 5000, group = gE, tooltip = "Diversification only works if the legs are actually uncorrelated. This correlates the five legs’ bar-by-bar exposure (-1 flat +1) pairwise and reports the average, alongside what the basket drawdown would have been had they moved as one.")

